\section{Paper identifier helpers}

Scholar's internal paper identity uses DOI when available, otherwise a hash of
title + first author's last name (see \cref{ch:paper-identity}).

In practice, papers often transition from \enquote{no DOI} to \enquote{has DOI}
after enrichment or when another provider returns additional metadata.
That transition would change the paper's stable ID and could orphan existing
notes/decisions saved under the legacy hash ID.

To keep this backward compatible, we expose the legacy hash-ID algorithm and a
small arXiv helper that can derive the canonical arXiv DOI
[[10.48550/arXiv.<id>]] from arXiv URLs.

\subsection{Legacy hash IDs}

The legacy hash ID matches the [[Paper.id]] implementation for papers without
DOIs: SHA-256 of normalized title + first author's last name, truncated to 16
hex characters.

<<utils functions>>=
import hashlib
import re


def legacy_hash_paper_id(title: str, authors: list[str] | None = None) -> str:
    """Return the legacy hash-based paper id.

    This mirrors [[Paper.id]] for papers without a DOI. It is used to migrate
    persisted notes/decisions when a paper later gains a DOI.
    """
    if title is None:
        title = ""
    if authors is None:
        authors = []

    title_normalized = title.lower().strip()

    author_part = ""
    if authors:
        first_author = authors[0] or ""
        last_name = first_author.split()[-1].lower() if first_author else ""
        author_part = last_name

    content = f"{title_normalized}|{author_part}"
    hash_value = hashlib.sha256(content.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
    return f"hash:{hash_value}"
@

\subsection{Surname-first names}
\label{sec:utils-surname-first}

The hash above takes the first author's surname to be the \emph{last}
word of the name.
That holds for every provider but Scopus, which writes names surname
first: \enquote{Fares E.}.
Hashed as it comes, such a name contributes its initial
(\enquote{e.}) instead of its surname, and the paper can never be
matched with the same paper from another provider, nor with its
preprint in the version-aware deduplication, which groups by this hash.

[[given_name_first]] restores the expected order.
It moves trailing initials to the front, and only those: a name that
does not end in initials (a mononym, a consortium) is not ours to
rearrange.
Particles need no special care, since they stay with the surname on
either side of the move.
It lives here rather than with the Scopus provider because the review
module needs it too, to recognise the entries that older sessions stored
in Scopus's order (\cref{sec:review-surname-first}).

<<utils functions>>=
TRAILING_INITIALS = re.compile(r"^(?:[^\W\d_]\.-?)+$")


def given_name_first(name: str) -> str:
    """Turn a surname-first `Surname I.` into `I. Surname`.

    Only trailing initials are moved, so `van der Berg J.H.` becomes
    `J.H. van der Berg` and a name without them is returned unchanged.
    """
    parts = name.split()
    split = len(parts)
    while split > 1 and TRAILING_INITIALS.match(parts[split - 1]):
        split -= 1
    return " ".join(parts[split:] + parts[:split])
@

<<test functions>>=
class TestGivenNameFirst:
    """Surname-first names are rearranged, all others left alone."""

    def test_moves_only_trailing_initials(self):
        for surname_first, expected in [
            ("Fares E.", "E. Fares"),
            ("Bodeveix J.P.", "J.P. Bodeveix"),
            ("Smith J.-P.", "J.-P. Smith"),
            ("van der Berg J.H.", "J.H. van der Berg"),
            ("Öztürk Ş.", "Ş. Öztürk"),
            ("Plato", "Plato"),
            ("ATLAS Collaboration", "ATLAS Collaboration"),
            ("Donald E. Knuth", "Donald E. Knuth"),
            ("E.", "E."),
            ("", ""),
        ]:
            assert given_name_first(surname_first) == expected

    def test_restores_the_surname_in_the_hash(self):
        title = "Correct pattern-based development"
        assert legacy_hash_paper_id(
            title, [given_name_first("Fares E.")]
        ) == legacy_hash_paper_id(title, ["Elie Fares"])
