\subsection{An abstract stored as paragraphs}
\label{sec:abstract-paragraphs}

[[Paper.abstract]] is declared [[str | None]], but Web of Science's
Expanded API gives an abstract of several paragraphs as several
[[<p>]] elements, which its XML-to-dict conversion turns into a
\emph{list} of strings (see the conversion of its records in the providers
module).
The provider has joined them since 2026-03-03 (commit 311e42e), but a
paper stored before then, or served from a search cache filled before
then, can still hold the list, and the first run of the abstract check
over real sessions met 77 such records
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/290}).
Code that treats the abstract as text then fails on them (the check
itself did: a list has no [[split]]).

[[join_paragraphs]] makes one text of such a list, the paragraphs
joined by a blank line, as the provider joins them: the blank line
keeps the paragraph breaks for a reader, and everything that wants one
line (the escapers, the check) collapses white space anyway.
Parts that are not strings are written as strings and empty parts are
dropped; a list with nothing in it is no abstract, [[None]].
Any other value passes through unchanged.
It is applied in three places: by the provider, where the list arises;
by [[Paper]] at construction, so that a session stored with a list is
repaired when it is loaded and saved back as text, as a list-valued
venue is (\cref{sec:paper-venue-invariant} in the core module); and by
[[clean_paper_text]] at ingest, because a paper unpickled from a
provider's cache is restored without passing through its constructor.

The other text fields were checked by reading the code: titles, venues
and identifiers are read as single values by every provider except
DBLP's venues, which [[normalize_venue]] already joins; the calibration
tool counts any stored field that is not text
(\cref{sec:check-abstracts-command} in the enrichment module), so a
real session would show one if it existed.

<<utils functions>>=
PARAGRAPH_JOINER = "\n\n"


def join_paragraphs(value: Any) -> Any:
    """Return a list or tuple of paragraphs as one text.

    The non-empty parts, as strings, joined by a blank line; None when
    there are none. Any other value is returned unchanged.
    """
    if isinstance(value, (list, tuple)):
        parts = [str(part).strip() for part in value if part is not None]
        return PARAGRAPH_JOINER.join(part for part in parts if part) or None
    return value
@

Let's verify the joining, and that the ingest cleaning joins a list
that reached a paper without passing its constructor, as an unpickled
one does.

