\subsection{Abstracts that are marked-up documents}
\label{sec:markup-to-text}

[[clean_metadata_text]] is made for titles: it removes a fixed list of
inline tags and keeps every other angle bracket.
The abstract services of enrichment hand over more than that.
Crossref's abstracts are JATS XML, paragraphs and sections and
formulas (\cref{sec:crossref-abstract}), and Europe PMC's may carry
HTML such as headings and line breaks (\cref{europepmc-module}); the
fixed list keeps [[<p>]], [[<h4>]] and [[<br/>]].
[[markup_to_text]] turns such a document into the words a reader sees,
and both services use it, so they agree on what an element becomes:
\begin{description}
\item[Block elements] (paragraphs, sections, titles, list items,
  headings, line breaks, table cells, in JATS or HTML spelling, with
  any namespace prefix) separate pieces of text, so each becomes a
  space; removing them outright would glue the last word of one
  paragraph to the first of the next, or the two words around a
  [[<jats:break/>]].
\item[Inline elements] sit inside a word as often as between words
  ([[H<jats:sub>2</jats:sub>O]]), so they are removed without a trace.
\item[Alternatives] A JATS [[<alternatives>]] element holds one formula
  several times, typically as MathML and as \TeX{} source; keeping all
  of them would print the formula twice.  We keep the MathML version
  when there is one, since its text is the formula's symbols in order,
  while the \TeX{} version is often a whole document with a preamble.
\end{description}
The character entities ([[&lt;]], [[&amp;]]) are decoded after the
tags are gone, so that a decoded [[<]] is never taken for the start of
a tag, and white space is collapsed last.

What counts as a tag is an opening angle bracket followed directly by a
letter (or by a slash and a letter) and closed by [[>]] before the next
[[<]].
In XML a literal [[<]] is always written [[&lt;]], so a JATS abstract
holds no other angle brackets.
A plain-text abstract may: [[p<0.05]] and [[n > 3]] are left alone, but
a comparison written [[a<b and c>d]] would be taken for a tag and lose
its middle.
That is the limit of reading markup and plain text with one function.

<<utils functions>>=
# Block elements of JATS and HTML: each becomes a space.
MARKUP_BLOCK_TAGS = re.compile(
    r"</?\s*(?:[a-z]+:)?(?:p|sec|title|list|list-item|label|caption"
    r"|disp-quote|def-list|def-item|term|def|break|br|hr|div|li|ul|ol"
    r"|h[1-6]|blockquote|table|tr|td|th)\b[^<>]*>",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
# Any other element: removed. A tag starts with a letter after "<".
MARKUP_TAGS = re.compile(r"</?[A-Za-z][\w:.-]*(?:\s[^<>]*)?/?>")
MARKUP_ALTERNATIVES = re.compile(
    r"<(?:[a-z]+:)?alternatives\b[^<>]*>(.*?)</(?:[a-z]+:)?alternatives\s*>",
    re.IGNORECASE | re.DOTALL,
)
MARKUP_MATHML = re.compile(
    r"<(?:mml:)?math\b.*?</(?:mml:)?math\s*>", re.IGNORECASE | re.DOTALL
)


def _one_alternative(match: re.Match) -> str:
    """Keep the MathML version of a formula's alternatives, if any."""
    inner = match.group(1)
    mathml = MARKUP_MATHML.search(inner)
    return mathml.group(0) if mathml else inner


def markup_to_text(raw: str) -> str:
    """Return the plain text of a JATS or HTML marked-up text.

    Keeps one version of a formula's alternatives, turns block
    elements into spaces, removes inline ones, decodes entities and
    collapses white space.
    """
    text = MARKUP_ALTERNATIVES.sub(_one_alternative, raw or "")
    text = MARKUP_BLOCK_TAGS.sub(" ", text)
    text = MARKUP_TAGS.sub("", text)
    text = html.unescape(text)
    return " ".join(text.split())
@

Let's verify each rule on small made-up texts, since the point is what
happens to each kind of element.

