\subsection{Several files, all or nothing}
\label{sec:staged-outputs}

An export that writes a [[.csv]], a [[.tex]] and a [[.bib]] must not leave
some of them new and some old.  Renaming several files can never be one
atomic step, but we can make the dangerous part, producing the
content (where bugs and bad data strike), happen before anything
is touched.  [[staged_outputs]] hands the caller an empty staging directory
inside the target directory.  The caller's generators write their files
there under their final names.  Only when the block finishes without an
exception are the files moved into the target directory.  If the block
raises, the staging directory and everything in it is removed and the
target directory is exactly as before.

The staging directory sits inside the target directory for the same reason
the temporary file does: the renames stay on one file system.  Generators
that derive sibling names from their output path (the \LaTeX{} report
writes [[<stem>.bib]] next to [[<stem>.tex]]) keep working unchanged, and
any reference one file makes to another uses the bare file name, which is
the same in both places.

The moves themselves can fail too.  A directory where a file should go
was the case seen (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/320}): the
first files of the export were renamed into place, the next rename
failed, and the export was left half new, which is what staging was for
to prevent.  So the last step is a small transaction of its own, in
three parts.
\begin{description}
\item[Check every target first] Before anything is moved, each target
  is checked for the reasons a rename refuses that can be seen in
  advance (\cref{sec:staged-outputs-check}).  A failure here moves
  nothing.
\item[Keep what is replaced] Each existing target is first moved aside
  into a hidden directory beside the staging, [[.scholar-replaced-*]],
  and only then is the new file moved in.  When a move fails part-way
  (a race with another program, a full disk), the moves made so far are
  undone, latest first: the old file is moved back over the new one, a
  new file that replaced nothing is removed.  The old files are deleted
  only when every move has succeeded.
\item[Say what is left] Only when undoing fails as well are the files
  left mixed, and then the error says, per file, whether it holds the
  new version, the earlier one or none, and where an earlier version
  that could not be put back is kept: the directory of replaced files
  is not removed in that case.
\end{description}
A caller may also ask for files to be removed, such as an earlier
export's file that the new one makes misleading.  A removal is part of
the same transaction: it is a move aside like the others, made after
every new file is in place, and undone with them.

What this guarantees rests on [[os.replace]], whose documentation says
that a successful rename is atomic (a POSIX requirement), that it
replaces an existing file silently \enquote{if the user has permission},
raises [[OSError]] for a non-empty directory, and \enquote{may fail if
src and dst are on different filesystems}
(\url{https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.html#os.replace}, read
2026-11-23).  Every rename here stays inside the target directory, so on
one file system.  What a file system cannot give is atomicity across
several names: between moving an old file aside and moving the new one
in, the name holds no file for a moment, and a reader at that instant
finds none (rename(2) promises that a single replacing rename leaves no
such moment, \url{https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/rename.2.html};
two renames cannot).  The session file avoids the gap by copying instead
(\cref{sec:atomic-write}), because other processes read it all the time;
an export's files are read by a person or a \LaTeX{} run started after
the export, so the gap is accepted for the sake of a restore that is a
rename too.  And if the process is killed between the moves, nothing
undoes them: the files moved so far stay, and the replaced ones stay in
the [[.scholar-replaced-*]] directory, whose files keep their names.

<<utils functions>>=
from collections.abc import Iterable

STAGING_PREFIX = ".scholar-staging-"
KEPT_PREFIX = ".scholar-replaced-"


@contextmanager
def staged_outputs(
    directory: str | os.PathLike, remove: Iterable[str] = ()
) -> Iterator[Path]:
    """Stage files and move them into `directory` only if all succeed.

    Yields a fresh staging directory inside `directory`. Write every
    output there under its final file name. When the block completes,
    each staged file is moved into `directory` and each file named in
    `remove` is removed from it, all or nothing as far as the file
    system allows: on a failure part-way the moves are undone, and
    `OutputsNotPlaced` says what each file holds. When the block
    raises, the staged files are discarded and `directory` is untouched.
    """
    directory = Path(directory)
    staging = Path(
        tempfile.mkdtemp(dir=directory, prefix=STAGING_PREFIX)
    )
    try:
        yield staging
        staged = sorted(p for p in staging.iterdir() if p.is_file())
        place_staged_files(directory, staged, remove)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(staging, ignore_errors=True)
@

[[place_staged_files]] is the transaction.  It works on two lists, the
targets of the staged files and the files to remove, and records before
moving anything whether each target held a file, which the error needs
to tell an earlier version from no file at all.  A removal of a file
that is not there is nothing to do, and a removal of a name a staged
file replaces anyway is already done by that move.  The staged files
are synced before the first move, as [[atomic_write_bytes]] syncs its
temporary file, so that no rename can become durable before the data it
points to.

<<utils functions>>=
def place_staged_files(
    directory: Path, staged: list[Path], remove: Iterable[str] = ()
) -> None:
    """Move each of `staged` into `directory` under its own name and
    remove the files named in `remove`, all or nothing as far as the
    file system allows. Raises `OutputsNotPlaced` on failure.
    """
    targets = [directory / path.name for path in staged]
    names = {path.name for path in staged}
    removals = [
        directory / name for name in remove
        if name not in names and os.path.lexists(directory / name)
    ]
    every_target = targets + removals
    existed = {target: os.path.lexists(target) for target in every_target}
    placement = _Placement(directory)
    <<check every target before moving anything>>
    try:
        for path in staged:
            with open(path, "rb") as f:
                os.fsync(f.fileno())
    except OSError as error:
        raise placement.failure(
            _failure_reason(error), every_target, existed
        ) from error
    <<move every file, undoing all moves on a failure>>
    placement.commit()


def _failure_reason(error: BaseException) -> str:
    """The exception as one line: its class and its message."""
    return f"{type(error).__name__}: {error}"
@

A failure while moving is first undone, then reported.  An interrupt
(Ctrl-C) is undone the same way but passes on as itself, since the
caller handles it as an interrupt; if undoing it failed, a warning says
what is left, because nothing else would.

<<move every file, undoing all moves on a failure>>=
try:
    for source, target in zip(staged, targets):
        placement.put(source, target)
    for target in removals:
        placement.take_away(target)
except BaseException as error:
    moved = placement.moved()
    undone = placement.roll_back()
    failure = placement.failure(
        _failure_reason(error), every_target, existed,
        rolled_back=moved and undone,
    )
    if not isinstance(error, Exception):
        if failure.changed:
            logging.getLogger(__name__).warning("%s", failure)
        raise
    raise failure from error
