\paragraph{The error.}
[[OutputsNotPlaced]] carries the outcome per target, in four lists, so
that a caller can word its own message from what happened instead of
from what it hoped.  [[changed]] is the one question most callers ask:
whether any target differs from before the call.  A target that held no
file before and holds none now has not changed, so it is listed
apart, as not written, rather than as missing.

<<utils functions>>=
class OutputsNotPlaced(Exception):
    """Staged outputs that could not all be moved into place.

    `new` lists the targets that now hold the staged file, `old` those
    that hold what they held before, `missing` those that held a file
    before and hold none now, and `not_written` those that held no file
    before and still hold none. `kept` maps a target to where its
    earlier version is kept when it could not be put back. `rolled_back`
    is true when files had been moved and every move was undone.
    """

    def __init__(
        self, reason: str, *, new=(), old=(), missing=(),
        not_written=(), kept=None, rolled_back: bool = False,
    ):
        super().__init__(reason)
        self.reason = reason
        self.new = list(new)
        self.old = list(old)
        self.missing = list(missing)
        self.not_written = list(not_written)
        self.kept = dict(kept or {})
        self.rolled_back = rolled_back

    @property
    def changed(self) -> bool:
        """Whether any target differs from what it was before."""
        return bool(self.new or self.missing)

    def file_lines(self) -> list[str]:
        """Say which target holds what, one line per kind there is."""

        def names(targets):
            return ", ".join(
                f"{target} (earlier version kept as {self.kept[target]})"
                if target in self.kept else str(target)
                for target in targets
            )

        kinds = (
            ("Holding the new version", self.new),
            ("Holding the earlier version", self.old),
            ("Missing", self.missing),
            ("Not written", self.not_written),
        )
        return [f"{label}: {names(targets)}"
                for label, targets in kinds if targets]

    def __str__(self) -> str:
        if self.changed:
            return "; ".join([self.reason, *self.file_lines()])
        if self.rolled_back:
            return f"{self.reason} (every file moved was put back)"
        return self.reason
@

\paragraph{Moving with a way back.}
[[_Placement]] makes the moves and remembers each one, so that it can
undo them and say afterwards where each target stands.  The directory of
replaced files is made only when a target exists, so an export into an
empty directory creates none.  A replaced file keeps its own name in it;
the names are unique, being names in one directory.  Undoing goes
latest first, so a target is always put back to the state before its
own move, and it goes on past a step that fails, so that one stubborn
file does not keep the others from being restored.

<<utils functions>>=
@dataclass
class _Move:
    """One target of a placement and how far its move got."""

    target: Path
    existed: bool
    aside: Path | None = None
    placed: bool = False


class _Placement:
    """Moves files into `directory`, keeping what they replace."""

    def __init__(self, directory: Path):
        self.directory = directory
        self.kept: Path | None = None
        self.moves: list[_Move] = []

    <<placement methods>>
@

Putting a file in place is two renames: the old file aside, the new one
in.  Taking a file away is the first of them alone.  A file to take away
that has gone meanwhile is what was wanted, so it ends that step
without error.

<<placement methods>>=
def put(self, source: Path, target: Path) -> None:
    """Move `source` to `target`, setting an existing target aside."""
    move = _Move(target, existed=os.path.lexists(target))
    self.moves.append(move)
    if move.existed:
        self._set_aside(move)
    os.replace(source, target)
    move.placed = True

def take_away(self, target: Path) -> None:
    """Set `target` aside, to be deleted once everything succeeded."""
    move = _Move(target, existed=True)
    self.moves.append(move)
    try:
        self._set_aside(move)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        if os.path.lexists(target):
            raise

def _set_aside(self, move: _Move) -> None:
    if self.kept is None:
        self.kept = Path(
            tempfile.mkdtemp(dir=self.directory, prefix=KEPT_PREFIX)
        )
    aside = self.kept / move.target.name
    os.replace(move.target, aside)
    move.aside = aside

def moved(self) -> bool:
    """Whether any file has been moved yet."""
    return any(move.placed or move.aside for move in self.moves)
@

[[roll_back]] puts an earlier version back with one rename, over the
new file if that was moved in, which is atomic for that name; a new file
that replaced nothing is removed.  Each failure is logged with its
reason, which the error's lists do not carry.  When everything was put
back, the directory of replaced files is empty and goes too.

<<placement methods>>=
def roll_back(self) -> bool:
    """Undo every move, latest first; whether all were undone."""
    undone = True
    for move in reversed(self.moves):
        try:
            if move.aside is not None:
                os.replace(move.aside, move.target)
                move.aside = None
            elif move.placed:
                os.unlink(move.target)
            move.placed = False
        except OSError as error:
            undone = False
            logging.getLogger(__name__).warning(
                "Could not restore %s: %s", move.target, error
            )
    if undone:
        self._drop_kept()
    return undone

def commit(self) -> None:
    """Make the moves durable and delete the replaced files."""
    _fsync_directory(self.directory)
    self._drop_kept()

def _drop_kept(self) -> None:
    if self.kept is None:
        return
    try:
        shutil.rmtree(self.kept)
    except OSError as error:
        logging.getLogger(__name__).warning(
            "Could not remove %s, which holds replaced files: %s",
            self.kept, error,
        )
@

The error is built from the moves: a target moved in holds the new
version, one set aside and not moved in holds none, and one never
touched (or put back) holds what it held before, a file or nothing.

<<placement methods>>=
def failure(
    self, reason: str, targets: list[Path], existed: dict,
    rolled_back: bool = False,
) -> OutputsNotPlaced:
    """An `OutputsNotPlaced` saying where each of `targets` stands."""
    moves = {move.target: move for move in self.moves}
    lists = {"new": [], "old": [], "missing": [], "not_written": []}
    kept = {}
    for target in targets:
        move = moves.get(target)
        if move is not None and move.aside is not None:
            kept[target] = move.aside
        if move is not None and move.placed:
            lists["new"].append(target)
        elif move is not None and move.aside is not None:
            lists["missing"].append(target)
        elif existed[target]:
            lists["old"].append(target)
        else:
            lists["not_written"].append(target)
    return OutputsNotPlaced(
        reason, kept=kept, rolled_back=rolled_back, **lists
    )
@

\paragraph{Checking the targets.}
\label{sec:staged-outputs-check}
Most refusals of a rename can be seen before it is tried, and a refusal
seen then costs nothing to undo.  [[output_target_problem]] looks for
three, from the errors rename(2) lists
(\url{https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/rename.2.html}, read
2026-11-23): a directory at the target (EISDIR; [[os.replace]] also
refuses a non-empty one, as quoted above), a directory without write
permission (EACCES), and the sticky-bit rule (EPERM): in a directory
with the sticky bit set, as [[/tmp]] usually is, only the owner of a
file or of the directory may replace or remove it.  Privileged processes
are exempt from that rule; Linux grants the exemption by the
[[CAP_FOWNER]] capability, which this check approximates by the root
user.

The checks are a forecast, not a guarantee.  [[os.access]] tests the
real user, not the effective one, unless asked (and the platform
supports it), and its documentation warns that \enquote{I/O operations
may fail even when [[access()]] indicates that they would succeed},
for network file systems in particular, and that the state can change
between the check and the use
(\url{https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.html#os.access}, read
2026-11-23).  That is why the moves are still undone on a failure: the
check only makes the undoing rarer.

<<check every target before moving anything>>=
problems = [
    problem for target in every_target
    if (problem := output_target_problem(target)) is not None
]
if problems:
    raise placement.failure("; ".join(problems), every_target, existed)
